<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johan Harmen Rudolf Köhler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1873">1873</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johan Harmen Rudolf Köhler was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> major-general who led the first expedition against the Aceh Sultanate in April 1873, opening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh-war">Aceh War</a>. His force landed near the capital but met fierce resistance, and Köhler was shot dead near the Great Mosque, the Masjid Raya Baiturrahman, on 14 April 1873. The expedition withdrew, and a larger second expedition under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-van-swieten">Jan van Swieten</a> followed later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-harmen-rudolf-kohler.jpg" alt="Johan Harmen Rudolf Köhler" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P.H.K. van Schendel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Generaal_Kohler.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Köhler was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a> on 3 July 1818. He enlisted in the 9th Infantry Division on 3 May 1832 and took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> that year, rising to corporal in 1834, fourier in 1836 and sergeant in 1838. On 14 November 1839 he left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>, having transferred to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-netherlands-east-indies-army">Royal Netherlands East Indies Army</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Four months after arriving in the Indies Köhler was made a second lieutenant, and he became a first lieutenant in 1847 and a captain in 1852. In the 1850s he governed Lampung, becoming Government Commissioner there in 1856, and on 7 January 1857 he received the Military Order of William for his service in Lampung. He was promoted to major in June 1858 while on leave, commanded the 2nd Infantry Battalion from 1859 and the Garrison Battalion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangka">Banka</a> from 1860, and rose to lieutenant colonel in 1862, colonel in 1865 and major general on 7 January 1873. He had married J. Beekhuis on 29 January 1859, and they had four children.</p><p>Köhler led a force of 3,000 in the First Aceh Expedition, sent to force the Aceh Sultanate to become a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> protectorate, with Eeldert Christiaan van Daalen as his deputy. The army reached Panté Ceureumèn on 6 April 1873 and the Baiturrahman Grand Mosque on 10 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Acehnese soldiers killed Köhler under a tree near the mosque on 14 April. The expedition lost 45 dead, 8 of them officers, and 405 wounded, and the campaign opened an Aceh War that went on for another thirty years. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-the-netherlands">William III of the Netherlands</a> offered his condolences to Köhler's family on a visit to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>. His soldiers could not carry his body away, and he was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a>; in 1954 his remains were reburied at the Peutjoet Cemetery. The tree under which he died was cut down in 2015.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Johan Köhler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their first expedition was repulsed and its commander, General J.H.R. <b>Köhler</b>, killed; a second, larger force under General Jan van Swieten took the sultan's palace in January 1874, and Sultan Mahmud Syah died of cholera soon afterwards.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banda-aceh">Banda Aceh</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.22,6.57&amp;year=1853" aria-label="Groningen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Johan Harmen Rudolf Köhler"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5336 5562h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5336 5562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.22,6.57&amp;year=1853">What was here in 1853? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
